#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082925 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "b293a58f-0903-5ee7-ab4b-3cbc2e5e4b3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-560" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of  the curve $y=x^{3/2}$ between the points $x=0$ and $x=\\dfrac{4}{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/assets/4ed8b94a4fff3c22697264e467f955f2e51a62618d03295c4e4f38493bff2ced.png)" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)= x^{3/2},$ $a=0,$ and $b=\\dfrac{4}{3}.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) =   \\dfrac{3}{2}x^{1/2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt { 1 + [f'(x)]^2 }\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{9}{4}x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4+9x}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}(4+9x)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}}\\sqrt{4+9x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4+9x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt { 1 + [f'(x)]^2 } = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4 + 9x}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\sqrt{4+9x}\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate this integral using the substitution $u= 4+9x.$ Then, $\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{9}\\textrm{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ u & 4 & 16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\sqrt{4+9x}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{16}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}{\\int }_{4}^{16}{u}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}{[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{\\frac{3}{2}}]}_{4}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{\\frac{3}{2}}]}_{4}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}([(16)^{\\frac{3}{2}}]-[(4)^{\\frac{3}{2}}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}([64]-[8]) \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082926 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "cf9adf09-b14f-5b5b-a110-60354b93c241" :question/math-academy-id "e-561" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\displaystyle  \\int_{5}^{6} } \\sqrt{1+\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve? \n\n- $y=  \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2-16}}$\n\n- $y=  \\sqrt{x^2-16} + \\sqrt 5$\n\n- $y=  \\dfrac{x}{x^2-16} + 1$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL=\\int_{5}^{6}  \\sqrt{1+\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[f'(x)]^2=\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f'(x) = \\pm \\dfrac {x}{\\sqrt{x^2-16}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-16}}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-16}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=  \\sqrt{x^2-16} + \\sqrt 5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083015 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length"}] :question/id #uuid "80794b09-e0f4-5bff-8c0c-8261638f9613" :question/math-academy-id "e-7268" :question/problem "What is the arc length of the curve $y = \\arccos{x}$ on $\\left[0,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right]?$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)= \\arccos{x} ,$ $a=0,$ and $b=\\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) =-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{\\frac{1-{x}^{2}+1}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{\\frac{2-{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60a38630-03f4-548c-b340-4fcde68748db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292720} {:answer/id #uuid "679f5fae-07a3-5117-bed0-8e4af0509409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+x)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292721} {:answer/id #uuid "e1343e64-0f5d-515c-b7db-f4566253a593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292722} {:answer/id #uuid "1a7b8f63-23fe-501e-9400-8597d549cd94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292723} {:answer/id #uuid "c141f822-aa3a-58bc-bbe3-8a843ae2fe5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a7b8f63-23fe-501e-9400-8597d549cd94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292723} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c093e5c-eb32-58ef-8dd8-6dc41b84f5e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e86eee2a-2a36-501d-8092-4b903ca0e024" :question/math-academy-id "q-122390" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(2-x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(2-x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=\\frac{1}{(2-x)^{2}}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\frac{1}{2-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\frac{1}{2-x}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm (-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x))+C \\\\  & =∓\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x)+1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14180a72-795d-5d8c-8111-279d202e86e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289369} {:answer/id #uuid "d0707116-ef74-5197-963c-8c375cacd1cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+2{x}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289370} {:answer/id #uuid "08b6f25a-e9e9-59bd-9947-9ff12f815bb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289371} {:answer/id #uuid "e8eb275b-2f62-5869-988c-ba551a60558a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}/4}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289372} {:answer/id #uuid "5056685c-72c9-521e-8e1a-d9f478a23a69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/4}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14180a72-795d-5d8c-8111-279d202e86e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289369} :answer-field/id #uuid "01c8dc1d-a7d1-5f5d-b2d8-5a99fecec513" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e867f4c1-a3f1-54bb-a1ef-62ea49513b8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-15327" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/assets/431975da27fb1ff06baa520ef4f870cb34c879f384bfc5ddd774422f15fe7b57.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve $y=4{e}^{-{x}^{2}/4}$ on $[0,2],$ as shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=4{e}^{-{x}^{2}/4},$ $a=0,$ and $b=2.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4(\\frac{-2x}{4}){e}^{-{x}^{2}/4}=-2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/4}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=-2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/4}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{(-2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "020b498d-a6f3-5d0e-8bcc-c4d1314160b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289376} {:answer/id #uuid "67434fb4-5dc3-5d30-bb28-a614c8e4d1da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "020b498d-a6f3-5d0e-8bcc-c4d1314160b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289376} :answer-field/id #uuid "55985c38-3501-5c26-9909-0e2c44ca6dff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "931269a7-495c-5a1a-9a6c-8781a5275593" :question/math-academy-id "q-346846" :question/problem "Compute the arc length $L$ of $y=\\frac{1}{3}{({x}^{2}+2)}^{3/2}$ for $x\\in [2,5].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{({x}^{2}+2)}^{3/2},$ $a=2,$ and $b=5.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{({x}^{2}+2)}^{1/2}\\cdot 2x=x\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+2)}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[x\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+2)}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}={x}^{2}+1$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x)|}_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}(5)^{3}+(5)]-[\\frac{1}{3}(2)^{3}+(2)] \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4bf2e86-1ac0-50ad-a9d3-3a26e4b49ce1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^3-e^{-3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4bf2e86-1ac0-50ad-a9d3-3a26e4b49ce1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^3-e^{-3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289380} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd960b61-2dd2-5cdf-aae2-fb036f4e55a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "08fee865-501b-5e99-8db0-650bb5d40ada" :question/math-academy-id "q-352925" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{6}$ for $x\\in [0,1].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{6},$ $a=0,$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{e}^{6x}-2+{e}^{-6x}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{e}^{6x}+2+{e}^{-6x}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{({e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x})^{2}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{2}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{6})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{e}^{3(1)}-{e}^{-3(1)}}{6}]-[\\frac{{e}^{3(0)}-{e}^{-3(0)}}{6}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}({e}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6}-1}{6{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8851d870-2a19-5307-8c33-af68172b7885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{x^{2} + 1}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081447} {:answer/id #uuid "930d8239-b612-5ec9-a778-197ab94cb52f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}∫_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{x^{2} + 4}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081448} {:answer/id #uuid "af92e866-31e2-5edd-b805-514b00a549b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{16x^{2} + 1}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081449} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a99530-2687-5bcd-a1f4-d16e28d0d5b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}∫_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{4x^{2} + 1}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081450} {:answer/id #uuid "694d36ea-c4f4-5041-b3ac-e7176a8e723a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}∫_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{x^{4} + 1}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "930d8239-b612-5ec9-a778-197ab94cb52f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}∫_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{x^{2} + 4}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081448} :answer-field/id #uuid "db4fcd60-79e6-5854-b482-1b69ae6078d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081446}] :question/id #uuid "b2c7c9a6-db2e-5889-8918-df15ae7908cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-51267" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/760/Source/Images/q-51267.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve $y = \\frac{x^{2}}{4}$ for $x ∈ [- 3, 4]$, as shown above?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcce3f8a-e74f-5250-9691-e7b13f82c71e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289390} {:answer/id #uuid "44237b70-f068-5b2e-bc85-b7f7e868b999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{1+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289391} {:answer/id #uuid "9632369b-eb7a-5e68-894e-d9071e3485ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{1+4x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289392} {:answer/id #uuid "4d078cc5-0265-52d9-81b0-8d51d81385ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289393} {:answer/id #uuid "c32a732c-57f3-5692-8181-a4342258fe61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcce3f8a-e74f-5250-9691-e7b13f82c71e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289390} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4f98978-f5bb-5067-9b81-51c09b8ac739" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9fad5ced-1622-5289-8903-0b749a22e8fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-5984" :question/problem "The length of the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ on $[2,4]$ is given by" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=2{x}^{2},$ $a=2,$ and $b=4.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=4x$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{1+(4x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "199910d0-a546-5dbf-8303-d453dd3a05b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292730} {:answer/id #uuid "2900ee99-267d-5ede-851f-464024e478be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292731} {:answer/id #uuid "59128ef1-2e85-565b-b109-06722de7a1d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=10x-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292732} {:answer/id #uuid "0364b99a-2a69-50fb-a319-287a64ee583d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{x}^{5}+\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292733} {:answer/id #uuid "761083cb-de89-58d8-b1dd-f3a75f56f9be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}+x+\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2900ee99-267d-5ede-851f-464024e478be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292731} :answer-field/id #uuid "f44ccde7-5000-5d85-98d7-850418ec4789" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0027455e-0f8d-5d58-b3c8-8d6a1a10e5aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-5985" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{3}^{5}\\sqrt{1+25{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{3}^{5}\\sqrt{1+25{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=25{x}^{4}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm 5{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm 5\\int {x}^{2}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{11}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac8a4ea9-cd1f-575b-acc6-2518eec4f4f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{3}{2}e^{2x} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081819} {:answer/id #uuid "be15bad8-ab4f-5eee-9c4d-d33beb010987" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{2}e^{2x} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081820} {:answer/id #uuid "e89e3846-fe1b-5de9-9901-dc5003382e00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{3}{2}e^{2x} + \\frac{e^{x}}{2} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081821} {:answer/id #uuid "fa17ed17-c008-5ba8-b071-1337c8be8de3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{2}e^{4x} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081822} {:answer/id #uuid "12612b39-9b02-5941-bc7e-3ab1a972ed67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{3}{2}e^{4x} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac8a4ea9-cd1f-575b-acc6-2518eec4f4f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{3}{2}e^{2x} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081819} :answer-field/id #uuid "33a47d8d-5346-5191-a099-0c2fdb2f5314" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081818}] :question/id #uuid "74becc4a-8ddf-5d15-9e77-edc438c95718" :question/math-academy-id "q-6002" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y = f(x)$ is given by $∫_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{1 + 9e^{4x}}dx$. Which of the following could be an equation for this curve?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cbf1d97-af21-5ce8-9db7-0e7e6d0dd02b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3\\sqrt{2-x}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289411} {:answer/id #uuid "425f803d-de1c-5a31-9348-7f5f474df2ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{2-x}+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289412} {:answer/id #uuid "289689a7-6d13-5736-89c1-c8f6fa89b554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289413} {:answer/id #uuid "a571a3cd-402d-5d2b-9ae3-5fb4210f78d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}-x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289414} {:answer/id #uuid "23259fe4-6729-5e8d-b710-22f8a82fbec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23259fe4-6729-5e8d-b710-22f8a82fbec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289415} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3c78f87-61b8-5747-a282-bde57adaa918" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ecf6b6f7-3aff-5153-b0cd-275806dc9892" :question/math-academy-id "q-6003" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm (-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})+C \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}+\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "497c0c31-1c23-5072-a25f-91b03bb2ded3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(4x)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289425} {:answer/id #uuid "58310cad-4b3d-5b2a-92ef-a3f61859ea16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{sec}^{2}⁡(2x)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289426} {:answer/id #uuid "72d1741f-2f12-56f8-825c-63e9baddf4c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289427} {:answer/id #uuid "dfcb29c1-0ed0-54e3-bb56-70f8288055ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289428} {:answer/id #uuid "2f88ac32-668d-59bd-91f0-4b1f208bc85b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{tan}^{2}⁡(2x)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72d1741f-2f12-56f8-825c-63e9baddf4c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289427} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9e1660e-6bb7-558c-a5e4-d5db08b6b790" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e5d5028e-993e-5a50-9b51-1e2b2f23dd9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-82615" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+4{sec}^{4}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+4{sec}^{4}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=4{sec}^{4}⁡(2x)\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm 2{sec}^{2}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm 2\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(2x)\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)+1.$"}]}
